        <metadata:prefixIRI>ND:0001061</metadata:prefixIRI>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A diagnosis which indicates that a disease is hypothosized to inhere in an organsim.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>TO DO</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>diagnosis of disease</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0001062 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0001062">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OGMS_0000073"/>
        <metadata:prefixIRI>ND:0001062</metadata:prefixIRI>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A diagnosis which indicates that a syndrome is hypothosized to inhere in an organsim.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:comment>Is the presence of a syndrome hypothosized?  Since a syndrome is a pattern of signs and symptoms, which are by definition observed or reported, is there a need to hypothesize their occurrence?  Perhaps the pattern can be hypothesized, but this too will typically (if not always) be observed or reported.

Perhaps the definition should be changed to: &quot;A diagnosis which indicates that a syndrome is exhibited by an organsim.&quot;?</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>diagnosis of syndrome</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0001063 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0001063">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OGMS_0000073"/>
        <metadata:prefixIRI>ND:0001063</metadata:prefixIRI>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A diagnosis which indicates that a disorder is either observed to inhere or hypothosized to inhere in an organsim.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>diagnosis of disorder</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003000 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003000">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003004"/>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003001 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003001">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OGMS_0000086"/>
        <metadata:prefixIRI>ND:0003001</metadata:prefixIRI>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Parkinsonism is the co-occurence of any combination of the classical features of Parkinson&apos;s disease [tremor, rigidity, akinesia/bradykinesia, postural instability].  It typically results from the realization of either Parkinsons disease, progressive supranuclear palsy, or multiple system atrophy.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>Parkinsonian syndrome</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>idiopathic parkinsonism</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>Parkinsonism</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003002 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003002">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003000"/>
        <metadata:prefixIRI>ND:0003002</metadata:prefixIRI>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Typical Alzheimers syndrome is the insidious onset of dementia syndrome that is characterized by episodic memory loss.  It typically results from the realization of Alzheimers disease.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>typical Alzheimers syndrome</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003003 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003003">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003001"/>
        <metadata:prefixIRI>ND:0003003</metadata:prefixIRI>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Parkinson plus syndrome is the co-occurence of any combination of the classical features of Parkinsons disease [tremor, rigidity, akinesia/bradykinesia, postural instability] with additional features [supranuclear palsy, paralysis of downward gaze, autonomic dysfunction, additional cortical signs, etc] that distinguishes it from simple idiopathic Parkinsons disease.  It typically results from the realization of either multiple system atrophy, progressive supranuclear palsy, corticobasal degeneration, or Lewy body dementia.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>atypical parkinsonian</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>disorder of multiple system degeneration</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>Parkinson plus syndrome</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003004 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003004">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OGMS_0000086"/>
        <metadata:prefixIRI>ND:0003004</metadata:prefixIRI>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Mild cognitive impairment is the occurence of one or more cognitive deficits [visual spatial, attention, orientation, executive function, functional] that are beyond those expected based on the age and education of the individual, but which are not significant enough to interfere with their daily activities.  It is typically the result of _________ .</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>Remove last sentence of defintion. &quot;Typically&quot; doesn&apos;t ontologize well. Nor do fill-in-the-blanks ;-)

Mark Jensen
</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000116>Suggest removing &apos;mild&apos; from label. Dementia is not a type of mild cognitive impairment. A syndrome of cognitive impairment makes more sense and tracks far better with reality. 

A diagnosis of MCI can be non-pathological, ie- normal ageing related cognitive decline.

Mark Jensen</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Mark Jensen</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>MCI</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000232 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000428"/>
        <rdfs:label>mild cognitive impairment</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003005 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003005">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003000"/>
        <metadata:prefixIRI>ND:0003005</metadata:prefixIRI>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Atypical Alzheimers syndrome is the insidious onset of dementia syndrome that is typically characterized by the absence of episodic memory loss.  It typically results from the realization of either frontal variant of Alzheimers disease, logopenic aphasia, posterior cortical atrophy, or primary progressive nonfluent aphasia.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>atypical Alzheimers syndrome</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003007 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003007">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0000120"/>
        <metadata:prefixIRI>ND:0003007</metadata:prefixIRI>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>ALS without family history</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>sporadic ALS</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>See: http://www.alshopefoundation.org/understanding-als/types-of-als.php</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>sporadic amyotrophic lateral sclerosis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003008 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003008">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0000120"/>
        <metadata:prefixIRI>ND:0003008</metadata:prefixIRI>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Familial ALS cases comprise 5 to 10 percent of all cases of ALS. Familial ALS (FALS) is inherited as an autosomal dominant trait. Almost 20 % of people with FALS have damage (called a mutation) in the gene that codes for the protein Cu/Zn superoxide dismutase (SOD 1) located on chromosome 21. There are no differences between familial and sporadic ALS on neurological exam except for occasional sensory loss in people with FALS. Other genes that, when damaged, can lead to ALS, have been identified on chromosomes 2, 9, 15, 18 and the X chromosome.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>familial ALS</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>See: http://www.alshopefoundation.org/understanding-als/types-of-als.php</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>familial amyotrophic lateral sclerosis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003009 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003009">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0000120"/>
        <metadata:prefixIRI>ND:0003009</metadata:prefixIRI>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Western Pacific ALS occurs primarily on the island of Guam, the Kii Peninsula of Japan, and New Guinea. The people on Guam have the same clinical characteristics seen in sporadic ALS and also can have a dementia and Parkinsons like illness. The illness on Guam is the first to be tied to a toxin, the cycad nut. This nut contains a compound that is toxic to nerve cells called BMAA and is similar to glutamate. The nut is eaten by the fruit bat which was a delicacy among the Chamorro population of Guam. This allowed a very high concentration of the toxin to be eaten and it has been postulated but not proven, that this caused the disease. The fruit bat was eaten to extinction and now fruit bats for consumption are imported, the disease has all but disappeared!</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>Guamanian</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>western pacific ALS</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>See: http://www.alshopefoundation.org/understanding-als/types-of-als.php</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>western pacific amyotrophic lateral sclerosis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003010 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003010">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0000120"/>
        <metadata:prefixIRI>ND:0003010</metadata:prefixIRI>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Rare cases of adolescent motor neuron disease that are clinically indistinguishable from ALS have been reported. Onset is between ages twelve and sixteen. Although ALS can occur at young ages, these cases are probably different from sporadic ALS.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>juvenile ALS</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>See: http://www.alshopefoundation.org/understanding-als/types-of-als.php</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>juvenile amyotrophic lateral sclerosis</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003011 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003011">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0000120"/>
        <metadata:prefixIRI>ND:0003011</metadata:prefixIRI>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>This is a rare disorder seem in young men. In this variant there is localized atrophy of one arm associated with increased reflexes implicating the presence of upper and lower motor neuron damage. However, the disorder is a problem at the junction between the cervical spine and the skull where there is pressure on the cervical spinal cord. In order to detect it, MRI of the neck needs to be performed in different positions including neck flexion and extension.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119>See: http://www.alshopefoundation.org/understanding-als/types-of-als.php</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label>hiramaya disease</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003012 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0003012">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ND_0000139"/>
        <metadata:prefixIRI>ND:0003012</metadata:prefixIRI>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>JP</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>PARK2</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>PDJ</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>Parkinson disease 2</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>autosomal recessive juvenile Parkinsonism</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>early onset Parkinson disease</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000118>juvenile Parkinsonism</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label>autosomal recessive juvenile Parkinson disease</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


