        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000423"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A mental function that is manifested through the cognitive activity of a thinking entity.

*** REVIEW ***</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116 xml:lang="en">NOTE: According to the Lezak text, subclasses of cognitive functions are divided into those that mediate verbal/symbolic information and those that deal with information that cannot be communicated in words or symbols (e.g. complex visual or sound patterns).</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012027 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012027">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012026"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A cognitive function which, when realized, is the means through which previously stored information is lost by a thinking being.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">forgetting cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012028 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012028">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012036"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">An expressive cognitive function that is manifested through a linguistic process as part of some linguistic cognitive activity of a thinking entity.

*** REVIEW ***</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">linguistic cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012029 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012029">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012026"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A cognitive function that is manifested through the visual/spatial cognitive activity of a thinking entity.

*** REVIEW ***</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alecander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">visual/spatial cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012030 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012030">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012026"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A cognitive function that is manifested through the cognitive activity of a thinking entity with regard to that entity&apos;s orientation to time, place, or self.

*** REVIEW ***</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">cognitive orientation function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012031 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012031">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012068"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000423"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A function that is manifested through the mental activity of a thinking entity.

*** REVIEW ***</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116 xml:lang="en">The Mental Functioning Ontology (MF) does not currently attempt to define &apos;mental function&apos;.  Once MF has created a strong candidate definition, we will import their term to replace our own since our intention is to create an ontology that is capable of interoperability with existing ontologies.

Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000232 xml:lang="en">TO DO: Add external references to MF and GO terms.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">mental function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012032 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012032">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012031"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000111 xml:lang="en">emotional function</obo:IAO_0000111>
        <obo:IAO_0000112 xml:lang="en">The functions that are realized in the process of a person becoming happy, sad, depressed, etc.</obo:IAO_0000112>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000423"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A mental function that is manifested through the emotional activity of an entity.

*** REVIEW ***</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">This class was created to provide preliminary justification for making &apos;cognitive function&apos; a subclass of &apos;mental function&apos; instead of collapsing the two classes into one class.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">emotional function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012033 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012033">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012026"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A cognitive function which, when realized, is the means through which the abilities to select, acquire, classify, and integrate information are realized.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">receptive cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012034 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012034">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012026"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A cognitive function which, when realized, is the means through which information is stored or retrieved by a thinking being.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116 xml:lang="en">As many as 134 named types of memory have been proposed.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memory:

In psychology, memory is the processes by which information is encoded, stored, and retrieved.  Encoding allows information that is from the outside world to reach our senses in the forms of chemical and physical stimuli.  In this first stage we must change the information so that we may put the memory into the encoding process.  Storage is the second memory stage or process.  This entails that we maintain information over periods of time.  Finally the third process is retrieval.  This is the retrieval of information that we have stored. We must locate it and return it to our consciousness.  Some retrieval attempts may be effortless due to the type of information.

From an information processing perspective there are three main stages in the formation and retrieval of memory:

1. Encoding or registration (receiving, processing and combining of received information)
2. Storage (creation of a permanent record of the encoded information)
3. Retrieval, recall or recollection (calling back the stored information in response to some cue for use in a process or activity)</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">memory/learning cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012035 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012035">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012026"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A cognitive function which, when realized, involves the mental organization or reorganization of of information. Thinking functions are realized in any mental operation that relates 2 or more bits of information either implicitly or explicitly and includes such functions as computation, reasoning and judgment, concept formation, abstracting and generalizing, ordering, organizing, planning, and problem solving. Categories of thinking are divided according to the type of information being manipulated and the operation (e.g. verbal reasoning) being performed on that information.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">thinking function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">executive cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012036 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012036">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012026"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A cognitive function which, when realized, is the means through which information is communicated or acted upon. The expressive functions make up the sum of observable behavior and are the means through which mental activity is inferred. Expressive functions include speaking, drawing or writing, manipulating objects, physical gestures, and facial expressions or gestures. Common problems with expressive functions include apraxia, constructional disorders, and aphasia and dysphasia.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">expressive cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012037 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012037">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012033"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A receptive cognitive function which, when realized, involves an arousal process that triggers central registration leading to analysis, encoding, and integrative activities. Sensory functions include sight, hearing, touch, taste, and smell and may also include movement, space, balance, and effort.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">See: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sensory_Reception</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">sensory reception cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012038 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012038">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012033"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A receptive cognitive function which, when realized, involves active processing of sensations as well as their inhibition or filtering from consciousness. Perception functions include awareness, recognition, discrimination, patterning, and orientation</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perception:

Perception is the organization, identification, and interpretation of sensory information in order to fabricate a mental representation through the process of transduction, which sensors in the body transform signals from the environment into encoded neural signals.  All perception involves signals in the nervous system, which in turn result from physical stimulation of the sense organs.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">perception cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012039 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012039">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012034"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A memory/learning cognitive function which, when realized, involves a conscious and intentional recollection process.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">explicit memory function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Declarative_memory:

Declarative memory (sometimes referred to as explicit memory) is one of two types of long term human memory.  It refers to memories which can be consciously recalled such as facts and knowledge.  Its counterpart is known as non-declarative or Procedural memory, which refers to unconscious memories such as skills (e.g. learning to ride a bicycle).  Declarative memory can be divided into two categories: episodic memory which stores specific personal experiences and semantic memory which stores factual information.

There are two types of declarative memory.  Semantic memories are those that store general factual knowledge that is independent of personal experience.  Examples include types of food, capital cities, lexical knowledge (vocabulary), etc.  Episodic memories are those that store specific events such as attending a class or flying to France. Retrieval of these memories can be thought of as mentally reliving the past events they concern.  Episodic memory is believed to be the system that provides the basic support for semantic memory.

From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Explicit_memory:

Explicit memory is the conscious, intentional recollection of previous experiences and information.  People use explicit memory throughout the day, such as remembering the time of an appointment or recollecting an event from years ago.  Explicit memory involves conscious recollection, compared with implicit memory which is an unconscious, unintentional form of memory.  Remembering a specific driving lesson is an example of explicit memory, while improved driving skill as a result of the lesson is an example of implicit memory.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">declarative memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012040 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012040">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012039"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A declarative memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves holding large amounts of incoming information briefly in sensory store and is a selecting and recording process through which perceptions enter the memory system.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">sensory memory function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000232 xml:lang="en">***TO DO***

Possibly make this a subclass of &apos;memory processing function&apos;.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Is this the same as working memory?

From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Working_memory:

Working memory has been defined as the system which actively holds information in the mind to do verbal and nonverbal tasks such as reasoning and comprehension, and to make it available for further information processing.  Working memory tasks are those that require the goal-oriented active monitoring or manipulation of information or behaviors in the face of interfering processes and distractions.  Working memory can only retain a limited amount of information; however, its capacity can be increased by use of a method known as chunking.  The cognitive processes involved include the executive and attention control of short-term memory which provide for the interim integration, processing, disposal, and retrieval of information.  These processes are sensitive to age; working memory is associated with cognitive development and research shows that its capacity tends to decline with old age.  Working memory is a theoretical concept central both to cognitive psychology and neuroscience.  In addition, studies done on a neurobiological basis have proven that working memory can be linked to learning and attention.

Theories exist both regarding the theoretical structure of working memory and the role of specific parts of the brain involved in working memory.  Research identifies the frontal cortex, parietal cortex, anterior cingulate, and parts of the basal ganglia as crucial.  The neural basis of working memory has been derived from lesion experiments in animals and functional imaging upon humans.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">registration memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012041 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012041">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012039"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A declarative memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves the storage and retrieval of information for XXX days.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">short-term memory function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Short-term_memory:

Short-term memory (or &quot;primary&quot; or &quot;active memory&quot;) is the capacity for holding a small amount of information in mind in an active, readily available state for a short period of time.  The duration of short-term memory (when rehearsal or active maintenance is prevented) is believed to be in the order of seconds.  A commonly cited capacity is 7 ± 2 elements.  In contrast, long-term memory indefinitely stores a seemingly unlimited amount of information.  Short-term memory should be distinguished from working memory, which refers to structures and processes used for temporarily storing and manipulating information.

See also: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Working_memory</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">short term memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012042 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012042">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012041"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A short term memory cognitive function which, when realized, is the first stage of short term memory storage, which temporarily holds information gathered via the registration or sensory memory function.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">NOTE: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Immediate_memory redirects to working memory.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">immediate memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012043 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012043">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012041"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A short term memory cognitive function which is realized through any repetitive process that serves to lengthen the duration of a memory trace.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">memory rehearsal cognitive function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memory_rehearsal:

Memory rehearsal is a term for the role of repetition in the retention of memories, e.g., working memory rehearsal tasks.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">rehearsal memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012044 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012044">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012041"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A short term memory cognitive function which, when realized, results in short term information storage that lasts longer than information acquired through the immediate memory function by up to 2 days.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">medium-term memory function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">medium term memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012045 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012045">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012039"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A declarative memory cognitive function which, when realized, results in the acquisition, storage, and consolidation of new information that lasts longer than short term memory.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">learning function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">long-term memory function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">secondary memory function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long-term_memory:

Long-term memory (LTM) is memory in which associations among items are stored, as part of the theory of a dual-store memory model.  According to the theory, long-term memory differs structurally and functionally from working memory or short-term memory, which ostensibly stores items for only around 20–30 seconds and can be recalled easily.  This differs from the theory of the single-store retrieved context model that has no differentiation between short-term and long-term memory.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">long term memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012046 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012046">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012045"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A long term memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves acquiring information without direct effort.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">latent learning cognitive function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incidental_learning:

Latent learning is a form of learning that is not immediately expressed in an overt response; it occurs without obvious reinforcement to be applied later.  Latent learning is when an organism learns a new concept in its life, however, the knowledge is not immediately expressed.  Instead, it remains dormant, and may not be available to consciousness, until specific events/experiences might need this knowledge to be demonstrated.  For instance a child may observe a parent setting the table or tightening a screw, but does not act on this learning for a year; then he finds out later on that he knows how to do these.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">incidental learning cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012047 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012047">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012045"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A long term memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves the acquisition and storage of autobiographical memories.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autobiographical_memory:

Autobiographical memory is a memory system consisting of episodes recollected from an individual&apos;s life, based on a combination of episodic (personal experiences and specific objects, people and events experienced at particular time and place) and semantic (general knowledge and facts about the world) memory.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">autobiographical memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012048 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012048">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012047"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A autobiographical memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves the acquisition of and storage of autobiographical memories from recent events.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">recent autobiographical memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012049 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012049">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012047"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A autobiographical memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves the acquisition of and storage of autobiographical memories from long past events.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">remote autobiographical memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012050 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012050">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012039"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A declarative memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves retrieving stored information.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">remembering cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012051 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012051">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012050"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A remembering cognitive function which, when realized, involves retrieving stored information through an active complex search process.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recollection:

Recall in memory refers to the retrieval of events or information from the past.  Along with encoding and storage, it is one of the three core processes of memory. There are three main types of recall: free recall, cued recall and serial recall.  Psychologists test these forms of recall as a way to study the memory processes of humans and animals.  Two main theories of the process of recall are the Two-Stage Theory and the theory of Encoding Specificity.

The Austin Simonson theory states that the process of recall begins with a search and retrieval process, and then a decision or recognition process where the correct information is chosen from what has been retrieved.  In this theory, recognition only involves the latter of these two stages, or processes, and this is thought to account for the superiority of the recognition process over recall.  Recognition only involves one process in which error or failure may occur, while recall involves two.  However, recall has been found to be superior to recognition in some cases, such as a failure to recognize words that can later be recalled.

The theory of encoding specificity finds similarities between the process of recognition and that of recall.  The encoding specificity principle states that memory utilizes information from the memory trace, or the situation in which it was learned, and from the environment in which it is retrieved.  Encoding specificity helps to take into account context cues because of its focus on the retrieval environment, and it also accounts for the fact recognition may not always be superior to recall.

See also: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recall_%28memory%29</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">recall memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012052 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012052">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012050"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A remembering cognitive function which, when realized, involves retrieving stored information when a stimulus triggers awareness.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recognition_memory:

Recognition memory is a subcategory of declarative memory.  Essentially, recognition memory is the ability to recognize previously encountered events, objects, or people. When the previously experienced event is reexperienced, this environmental content is matched to stored memory representations, eliciting matching signals.  Recognition memory can be subdivided into two component processes: recollection and familiarity, sometimes referred to as &quot;remembering&quot; and &quot;knowing&quot;, respectively.  Recollection is the retrieval of details associated with the previously experienced event.  In contrast, familiarity is the feeling that the event was previously experienced, without recollection.  Thus, the fundamental distinction between the two processes is that recollection is a slow, controlled search process, whereas familiarity is a fast, automatic process.

Mandler&apos;s &quot;Butcher-on-the-bus&quot; example:

Imagine taking a seat on a crowded bus.  You look to your left and notice a man.  Immediately, you are overcome with this sense that you&apos;ve seen this man before, but you cannot remember who he is.  This automatically elicited feeling is familiarity. While trying to remember who this man is, you begin retrieving specific details about your previous encounter.  For example, you might remember that this man handed you a fine chop of meat in the grocery store.  Or perhaps you remember him wearing an apron.  This search process is recollection.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">recognition memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012053 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012053">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012039"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A declarative memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves the storage or retrieval of information of a specific type.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">material specific memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012054 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012054">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012053"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A material specific memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves the storage or retrieval of verbal information.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116 xml:lang="en">Explain what &quot;verbal inforamtion&quot; is. MPJ</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000116 xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Verbal_memory:

Verbal memory is a term used in cognitive psychology that refers to memory of words and other abstractions involving language.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">verbal memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012055 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012055">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012053"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A material specific memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves the storage or retrieval of nonverbal information.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">nonverbal memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012056 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012056">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012053"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A material specific memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves the storage or retrieval of information necessary for executing motor skills.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motor_skill:

A motor skill is a learned sequence of movements that combine to produce a smooth, efficient action in order to master a particular task.  The development of motor skill occurs in the motor cortex, the region of the cerebral cortex that controls voluntary muscle groups.  The stages to motor learning is the cognitive phase, the associative phase, and the autonomous phase.

Cognitive Phase: When a learner is new to a specific task, the primary thought process starts with, “what needs to be done?”  Considerable cognitive activity is required so that the learner can determine appropriate strategies to adequately reflect the desired goal.  Good strategies are retained and inefficient strategies are discarded.  The performance is greatly improved in a short amount of time.

Associative Phase: the learner has determined the most effective way to do the task and starts to make subtle adjustments in performance.  Improvements are more gradual and movements become more consistent.  This phase can last for a long time.

Autonomous Phase: this phase may take several months to years to reach.  The phase is dubbed because the performer can now “automatically” complete the task without having to pay any attention to it.  Examples include walking and talking or sight reading while doing simple arithmetic.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">motor skill learning cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012057 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012057">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012039"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A declarative memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves the storage or retrieval of information that is accessed through a specific sensory modality of testing [e.g. sight, hearing, touch, taste, or smell].</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116 xml:lang="en">Consider subtypes for each modality? MPJ</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modality_%28semiotics%29:

In semiotics, a modality is a particular way in which the information is to be encoded for presentation to humans, i.e. to the type of sign and to the status of reality ascribed to or claimed by a sign, text or genre.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">modality specific memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012058 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012058">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012039"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A declarative memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves the storage or retrieval of memories of one&apos;s own experiences. Episodic memory is contrasted with semantic memory.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">declarative memory function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">event memory function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000232 xml:lang="en">NOTE: This should probably be combined with the &apos;autobiographical memory function&apos;.

WARNING: There is a potential conflict with the alternative term &apos;declarative memory function&apos; for this class and its superclass with the same name!!!

NOTE: This is NOT the same thing as working memory.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Explicit_memory:

Episodic memory, a part of autobiographical memory, consists of the recollection of events in the life of a person.  These can be memories that happened to the person directly or just be memories of events that happened around them.  Simply put, it is the memory of life experiences centered on oneself.  Episodic memory is necessary for &quot;time traveling&quot;: remembering your past.  Episodic memory does not have any direct affect on how one imagines their future.  It is considered a uniquely human quality that depends on maturation and therefore won&apos;t be found in babies and young children.  For children, recollection of these memories can also be hindered by traumatic events that occur during childhood.

From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Episodic_memory:

Episodic memory is the memory of autobiographical events (times, places, associated emotions, and other contextual knowledge) that can be explicitly stated.  Semantic and episodic memory together make up the category of declarative memory, which is one of the two major divisions in memory.  Events that are recorded into episodic memory may trigger episodic learning, i.e. a change in behavior that occurs as a result of an event.  For example, a fear of dogs that follows being bitten by a dog is episodic learning.  One of the main components of episodic memory is the process of recollection.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">episodic memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012059 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012059">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012039"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A declarative memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves the storage or retrieval of the sorts of information that is learned as knowledge. Semantic memory is contrasted with episodic memory.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Explicit_memory:

Semantic memory consists of all explicit memory that is not autobiographical.  Examples of semantic memory is knowledge of historical events and figures; the ability to recognize friends and acquaintances; and information learned in school, such as specialized vocabularies and reading, writing and mathematics.

From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semantic_memory:

Semantic memory refers to the memory of meanings, understandings, and other concept-based knowledge unrelated to specific experiences.  The conscious recollection of factual information and general knowledge about the world is generally thought to be independent of context and personal relevance.  Semantic and episodic memory together make up the category of declarative memory, which is one of the two major divisions in memory.  With the use of our semantic memory we can give meaning to otherwise meaningless words and sentences.  We can learn about new concepts by applying our knowledge learned from things in the past.  Semantic memory includes generalized knowledge that does not involve memory of a specific event.  For instance, people can answer questions like &quot;Are wrenches pets or tools?&quot; without remembering any specific event in which they learned that wrenches are tools.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">semantic memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012060 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012060">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012039"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A declarative memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves the passive storage or retrieval of information. Automatic memory cognitive function is contrasted with effortful memory cognitive function.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">automatic memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012061 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012061">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012039"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A declarative memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves effortful processing of information for storage or retrieval. Effortful memory function is contrasted with automatic memory function.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">effortful memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012062 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012062">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012039"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A declarative memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves the storage or retrieval of information about when or where another piece of information was learned.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">source memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012063 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012063">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012039"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A declarative memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves remembering to do something at a specific time.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prospective_memory:

Prospective memory is a form of memory that involves remembering to perform a planned action or intention at the appropriate time.  Prospective memory tasks are highly prevalent in daily life and range from relatively simple tasks to extreme life-or-death situations.  Examples of simple tasks include remembering to put the toothpaste cap back on, remembering to reply to an email or remembering to return a rented movie.  Examples of highly important situations include a patient remembering to take medication or a pilot remembering to perform specific safety procedures during a flight.

In contrast to prospective memory, retrospective memory involves memory of people, events and words that have been encountered in the past.  Prospective memory and retrospective memory differ in the fact that retrospective memory emphasizes memory for events that have previously occurred, while prospective memory focuses on intended future events and is thus considered a form of memory for the future.  Retrospective memory involves the memory of what we know, containing informational content; prospective memory focuses on when to act, without focusing on informational content.  There is some evidence demonstrating the role of retrospective memory in the proper performance of prospective memory, but this role seems to be relatively small.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">prospective memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012064 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012064">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012034"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A memory/learning cognitive function which, when realized, involves the storage or retrieval of information that is expressed without the subject&apos;s phenomenal awareness that they possess it.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">implicit memory function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">non declarative memory function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Implicit_memory:

Implicit memory is a type of memory in which previous experiences aid in the performance of a task without conscious awareness of these previous experiences.  Evidence for implicit memory arises in priming, a process whereby subjects are measured by how they have improved their performance on tasks for which they have been subconsciously prepared.  Implicit memory also leads to the illusion-of-truth effect, which suggests that subjects are more likely to rate as true those statements that they have already heard, regardless of their veracity.  In daily life, people rely on implicit memory every day in the form of procedural memory, the type of memory that allows people to remember how to tie their shoes or ride a bicycle without consciously thinking about these activities.  Research into implicit memory indicates that it operates through a different mental process from explicit memory.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">nondeclarative memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012065 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012065">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012064"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A nondeclarative memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves motor and cognitive skill learning and perceptual &quot;how to&quot; learning.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">skill memory function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">From http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Procedural_memory:

Procedural memory is memory for how to do things.  Procedural memory guides the processes we perform and most frequently resides below the level of conscious awareness.  When needed, procedural memories are automatically retrieved and utilized for the execution of the integrated procedures involved in both cognitive and motor skills; from tying shoes to flying an airplane to reading.  Procedural memories are accessed and used without the need for conscious control or attention.  Procedural memory is a type of long-term memory and, more specifically, a type of implicit memory.  Procedural memory is created through &quot;procedural learning&quot; or, repeating a complex activity over and over again until all of the relevant neural systems work together to automatically produce the activity.  Implicit procedural learning is essential to the development of any motor skill or cognitive activity.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">procedural memory cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012066 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012066">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012064"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A nondeclarative memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves cued recall in which, without the subject&apos;s awareness, prior exposure facilitates the response.

Wikipedia: Priming is an implicit memory effect in which exposure to a stimulus influences a response to a later stimulus.  It can occur following perceptual, semantic, or conceptual stimulus repetition.  For example, if a person reads a list of words including the word table, and is later asked to complete a word starting with tab, the probability that he or she will answer table is greater than if not so primed.  Another example is if people see an incomplete sketch that they are unable to identify and they are shown more of the sketch until they recognize the picture, later they will identify the sketch at an earlier stage than was possible for them the first time.  The effects of priming can be very salient and long lasting, even more so than simple recognition memory.  Unconscious priming effects can affect word choice on a word-stem completion test long after the words have been consciously forgotten.  Priming works best when the two stimuli are in the same modality. For example visual priming works best with visual cues and verbal priming works best with verbal cues. But priming also occurs between modalities, or between semantically related words such as &apos;doctor&apos; and &apos;nurse&apos;.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">perceptual learning function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Priming_%28psychology%29</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <obo:IAO_0000232 xml:lang="en">Add positive and negative priming as subclasses:

From Wikipedia: The terms positive and negative priming refer to when priming affects the speed of processing.  A positive prime speeds up processing, while a negative prime lowers the speed to slower than un-primed levels.  Positive priming is caused by simply experiencing the stimulus, while negative priming is caused by experiencing the stimulus, and then ignoring it.  Positive priming effects happen even if the prime is not consciously seen.  The effects of positive and negative priming are visible in event-related potential (ERP) readings.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">priming cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012067 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012067">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012064"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A nondeclarative memory cognitive function which, when realized, involves learning in which one stimulus, the conditioned stimulus, comes to signal the occurrence of a second stimulus, the unconditioned stimulus.  The unconditioned stimulus is a biologically significant stimulus such as food or pain that elicits a response from the start; this is called the unconditioned response. The conditioned stimulus usually elicits no particular response at first, but after conditioning it elicits the conditioned response.

Classical conditioning is to be distinguished from operant or instrumental conditioning, in which behavior emitted by the organism is strengthened or weakened by its consequences (e.g. reward or punishment).</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116 xml:lang="en">Class adoption from: Chapter 2 of Lezak, et. al. (2004) /Neuropsychological Assessment/ 4th Ed.</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">Pavlovian conditioning cognitive function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">respondent conditioning cognitive function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Classical_conditioning</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">classical conditioning cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012068 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012068">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000034"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000423"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000232 xml:lang="en">Maybe this should be called &apos;biological function&apos; instead of &apos;bodily function&apos;.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">bodily function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012069 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012069">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012028"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A linguistic cognitive function which, when realized, is the means through which the name of some stimulus entity is called to mind and communicated.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexanbder P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">naming cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012070 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012070">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012028"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A linguistic cognitive function which, when realized, is the means through which a list of words is produced.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000232 xml:lang="en">Is this a function or should it be removed?</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">word generation cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012071 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012071">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012070"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A word generation cognitive function which, when realized, is the means through which a list of words that belong to a common semantic category is produced.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">linguistic category fluency cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012072 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012072">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012070"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A word generation cognitive function which, when realized, is the means through which a list of words that begin with a common letter is produced.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">linguistic letter fluency cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012073 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012073">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012028"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A linguistic cognitive function which, when realized, is the means through which words or sounds are mimicked.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">language repetition cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012074 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012074">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012028"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A linguistic cognitive function which, when realized, is the means through which information is communicated through the articulation of words or sounds.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">motor speech function</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">speech production cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012075 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012075">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012028"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A linguistic cognitive function which, when realized, is the means through which written or spoken information is understood.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">language comprehension cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012076 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012076">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012028"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A linguistic cognitive function which, when realized, is the means through which information is acquired through reading meaningful letters or characters.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000232 xml:lang="en">Consider making this a subclass of &apos;language comprehension cognitive function&apos; and add &apos;listening cognitive function&apos; as a sister class to it.</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">reading cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012077 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012077">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012028"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A linguistic cognitive function which, when realized, is the means through which information is communicated by producing meaningful letters or characters that constitute readable matter.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000116 xml:lang="en">Consider adding &apos;drawing cognitive function&apos;</obo:IAO_0000116>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">writing cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012078 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012078">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012030"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A cognitive orientation function which, when realized, is the means through which orientation to time (such as the current time of day, date, day of the week, season, month, or year) occurs.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">orientation to time</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">temportal cognitive orientation function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012079 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012079">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012030"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A cognitive orientation function which, when realized, is the means through which orientation to one&apos;s place (such as one&apos;s current location, place of residence, city, state, building, or floor) occurs.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">orientation to place</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">spatial cognitive orientation function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012080 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012080">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012030"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A cognitive orientation function which, when realized, is the means through which orientation to one&apos;s self (such as one&apos;s name, age, previous occupation, and identities of relatives) occurs.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000118 xml:lang="en">orientation to self</obo:IAO_0000118>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">personal cognitive orientation function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012081 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012081">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012026"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000114 rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115 xml:lang="en">A cognitive function which, when realized, results in a thinking entity maintaining continued cognitive focus on a specific entity.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117 xml:lang="en">Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <obo:IAO_0000232 xml:lang="en">Should this be made a subclass of executive cognitive function?</obo:IAO_0000232>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">attention cognitive function</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012082 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012082">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0012000"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000059"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0011071"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An assay plan to produce information about some evaluant&apos;s level of mental functioning that is the concretization of an assay design and inheres in a bearer who is committed to realizing it as a mental functioning assay.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>mental functioning assay plan</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013000 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013000">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0000186"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A quality of a single physical entity inhering in a bearer by virtue of the bearer&apos;s mental capacities as demonstrated by the bearer&apos;s responses to mental tasks or other situational stimuli.

*** REVIEW ***</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:comment>I am unsure whether there is such a thing as a &apos;mental quality&apos; or a &apos;cognitive quality&apos;. Every quality must inhere in some entity, but it is unclear what type of entity is the bearer of a mental quality. In regard to cognitive qualities, it seems natural to say that they are qualities of cognition, but cognition is best cashed out as a set of functions and the processes that realize those functions, but processes do not have qualities (they have &apos;process profiles&apos;) and functions are specifically dependent continuants and, as such, do not seem to be the appropriate type of entity to be the bearer of qualities. At second glance, it seems that mental and cognitive qualities must inhere in material entities and, more specifically, in things such as human brains. {NOTE- I would argue they inhere in a human organism, the patient -MARK JENSEN} This seems correct, but, while the complexity of the human brain gives rise to cognition, it does not seem appropriate to say that a certain configuration of parts of a brain is the bearer of a mental or cognitive quality since, cognition is a function or process that that configuration of parts of a brain participate in. Perhaps there is an acceptable quality that inheres in a brain, but it probably should not be called a &apos;mental&apos; or a &apos;cognitive&apos; quality.

Note the disanalogy between the relationship between so-called cognitive qualities and human brains versus the relationship between a physical quality such as the color green and its bearers such as a leaf.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>mental quality</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013001 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013001">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013000"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A quality of a single physical entity inhering in a bearer by virtue of the bearer&apos;s cognitive capacities as demonstrated by the bearer&apos;s responses to cognitive tasks or other situational stimuli.

*** REVIEW ***</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>cognitive quality</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013002 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013002">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013006"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A cognitive quality that represents that its bearer&apos;s current cognitive functioning is comparable to the level of cognitive functioning that a healthy member of that type of entity bears.

*** REVIEW ***</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>normal cognition</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013003 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013003">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013006"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A cognitive quality that represents that its bearer&apos;s current cognitive functioning is mildly impaired in comparison to the level of cognitive functioning that a healthy member of that type of entity bears.

*** REVIEW ***</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>mildly impaired cognition</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013004 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013004">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013006"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A cognitive quality that represents that its bearer&apos;s current cognitive functioning is moderately impaired in comparison to the level of cognitive functioning that a healthy member of that type of entity bears.

*** REVIEW ***</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>moderately impaired cognition</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013005 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013005">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013006"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A cognitive quality that represents that its bearer&apos;s current cognitive functioning is severely impaired in comparison to the level of cognitive functioning that a healthy member of that type of entity bears.

*** REVIEW ***</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:label>severely impaired cognition</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013006 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0013006">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/NPT_0011001"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A cognitive quality that represents the degree to which its bearer&apos;s current cognitive functioning is representative of the level of cognitive functioning that a healthy member of that type of entity bears.

*** REVIEW ***</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <obo:IAO_0000117>Alexander P. Cox</obo:IAO_0000117>
        <rdfs:comment>*** NOTE from Alexander P. Cox ***

This class was created as part of the initial attempt to represent the entities that cognitive function measurement data are about.  Later considerations nearly led me to delete this class and all mental quality classes due to a theoretical issue concerning what mental qualities are qualities of.  (See the comment for mental quality for more info on this.)  However, it seems clear that, if there is such a thing as a level of cognitive functioning, these levels are in fact qualities and are most likely qualities of cognitive functions (or the processes that realize them).

Another question arises as to whether a cognitive function is the sort of thing that can have levels.  There are at least 2 ways that we can understand an affirmative answer to this question.  First, it may be the case that cognitive functions can be partially realized in cognitive processes.  If this is the case, then we might say that a function that gets 100% realized has the normal level of functioning quality.  Similarly, a function that gets 95% realized has the mildly impaired level of functioning quality, and so on for the other levels of functioning, which will be assigned ranges of their % of realization.  The presumption here is that we can indirectly measure the degree of realization of a function by measuring the degree of completion of the process that realizes it.  There may, of course, be reasons other than an impairment of a function as to why a process is not fully realized, but we can set this issue aside for now.  According to this approach for representing qualities of functions, a function is the sort of thing that comes in degrees and can be partially realized.

It may be objected that functions are binary in the sense that their realizations are an all or nothing sort of thing.  That is, there is no degree of realization between 0 and 1.  This view requires an alternative method of assessment in order to generate levels of cognitive functioning.  One solution is to have levels of cognitive functions be qualities of a set of instances of the cognitive process that realizes the cognitive function in question.  We can test whether functions are realized or not by observing whether their respective processes are successful or not.  A process is successful if and only if it produces the correct output for the given input.  Otherwise, the process is unsuccessful.  Levels of functioning are assigned on the basis of the percentage of successful processes which is generated by dividing the number of instances of successful processes by the number of attempted processes.  Thus, for a given function, a 95% or higher success rate might be representative of a normal level of functioning for that function.  An 85-94% success rate might be representative of a mildly impaired level of functioning for that function.  And so on for each quality.  The success rates associated with each level of functioning will presumably vary from function to function as well as in relation to the nature and difficulty of the task assigned to test each function.  For example, a memory assay that requires a test subject to repeat 3 common words after hearing them once will probably demand 100% success to determine that the normal functioning quality is present.  This is contrasted with a similar memory assay that requires a test subject to repeat 30 common words after hearing them once.  The latter assay is significantly more difficult and may only demand a 33% or better success rate to determine that the normal functioning quality is present.  The exact details regarding which success rates mark the cut-offs for the various levels of functioning should be left up to the creators of each assay and need not concern us here except insofar as it is assumed that such cut-offs exist and can be provided when necessary.

It is my contention that the second approach to understanding levels of cognitive functions (and qualities of functions in general) is preferable to the first.  There is, however, yet another question that may cause problems for this view.  Namely, if the realization of a function is all or nothing and a particular process does not successfully realize a function, is that process still an instance of the type of process that is supposed to realize that function?  For example, the recall process is the process through which the recall function is realized.  If the subject does not successfully recall the piece of information in question, then, according to this view, the recall function was not realized.  But, if the recall function wasn&apos;t realized, then the recall process must not have occurred.  This may cause a problem for the generation of success rates for functions since every process will, by definition, always be successful.  This is because the process only occurs if it realizes the function and the function is only realized if it is successful.  It follows that processes are always successful.  While this raises interesting questions concerning the nature of processes, functions, and their relationship to one another, I think that the problem for determining the success rate of functions is artificial.  What matters for the calculation of success rates is the number of times that the process is attempted or, at least, is supposed to occur.  The exact definition of an attempted process may need to be defined, but, for now, I expect that we can lean on our folk understanding of these matters to generate success rates.

It is worth noting that the above discussion is driven by considerations regarding relatively simple functions/processes.  That is, it assumes that the functions/processes receive an input, do their thing, and then, if successful, produce an output.  It is very likely that most functions/processes that we are concerned with are more complex than this and have many subprocesses that realize their respective subfunctions.  In fact, the folk understanding of a process arguably includes an assumption that the process has subprocesses that must occur in some sequence in order for the larger process to be completed.  For example, the process of walking down a flight of stairs will have subprocess for each step down as parts.  This understanding of processes lends itself better to the first approach of assigning the qualities of level of functioning.  In the stairs example, we might say that a person who successfully makes it down 19 steps before falling on the 20th and final step had a 95% success rate because they completed 95% of the walking down the stairs process.  While this is a natural way to look at the issue, it does not undermine the second proposed solution.  What occurs in the stairs example is a repetition of the taking a step down function.  To say that the process of walking down the stairs is 95% completed is not to say that the walking down the stairs function was 95% realized, but rather to say that the taking a step down function is successfully realized 19 out of 20 times.  According to this account, there is no walking down the stairs function; rather, there is a taking a step down function that can be realized multiple times during a larger process that has many instances of the taking a step down process as parts.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>*** QUESTION ***
Should this be labeled &apos;level of cognition&apos; instead?</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>level of cognitive functioning</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


