    <!-- http://scai.fraunhofer.de/AlzheimerOntology#DAD -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://scai.fraunhofer.de/AlzheimerOntology#DAD">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/OBI_0500027"/>
        <metadata:prefixIRI>AlzheimerOntology:DAD</metadata:prefixIRI>
        <NDDUO:Synonym>DAD</NDDUO:Synonym>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">The Clinical Dementia Rating or CDR is a numeric scale used to quantify the severity of symptoms of dementia (i.e. its &apos;stage&apos;). Using a structured-interview protocol, a qualified health professional assesses a patient&apos;s cognitive and functional performance in six areas: memory, orientation, judgment &amp; problem solving, community affairs, home &amp; hobbies, and personal care. Scores in each of these are combined to obtain a composite score ranging from 0 through 3. Clinical Dementia Rating Assignment Qualitative equivalences are as follows:NACC Clinical Dementia Rating

CDR is credited with being able to discern very mild impairments, but its weaknesses include the amount of time it takes to administer, its ultimate reliance on subjective assessment, and relative inability to capture changes over time.

--Wikipedia--</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>from manual annotation of text corpus</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Disability Assessment for Dementia</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


